5. Effects of surface roughness
The presence of surface roughness affects the laminar velocity profile and decreases the transitional Reynolds number. This has been shown by a number of experiments and a comprehensive review can be found elsewhere (Merkle et al., 1974; Tani, 1969). In the present work, based on Merkle's modified viscosity model the effects of the surface roughness on laminar flow in microtubes are considered in terms of a roughness-viscosity function. Generally, the roughness increases the momentum transfer in the boundary layer near the wall. This additional momentum transfer can be accounted for by introducing a roughness-viscosity μR in a manner similar to the eddy-viscosity concept in the turbulent flow model. Apparently, the roughness-viscosity μR should have a higher value near the wall and gradually diminish towards the center of the channel. The roughness-viscosity μR should also be proportional to Re. The ratio of the roughness-viscosity to the fluid viscosity is proposed to take the following form,
